artifact.self_contained_problem:
Let G=(V,E) be a (possibly directed) graph modelling a biological network, and let L: V → C be a labelling of the vertices by subcellular compartments drawn from a finite set C (for example, C ⊇ {cytosol, nucleus, mitochondrion, chloroplast, ...}). Assume each compartment c ∈ C is equipped with a prescribed planar region R(c) ⊂ ℝ² that reflects the topographical/morphological shape of that compartment inside an idealized cell, together with a nesting relation (some regions lie inside others), and assume a set of drawing conventions D for the network type (e.g. metabolic, signaling, regulatory) specifying allowed edge styles, node glyphs and admissible crossings.

Devise an algorithm that, given (G, L, {R(c)}, D), produces a 2-dimensional drawing φ: V → ℝ², together with curves for the edges, such that:
(i) for every v ∈ V, φ(v) lies in the region R(L(v)), and the boundaries of the regions R(c) appear as visible compartment boundaries in the drawing;
(ii) the drawing respects the conventions D;
(iii) if G is directed and represents a flow of mass or information, the drawing displays a consistent global flow direction (e.g. via a hierarchical layering of V into levels V_1, …, V_k with most edges going from lower to higher levels), while also permitting edges that bypass intermediate levels so that mass/information is not restricted to passing only between neighboring layers;
(iv) the drawing optimizes standard aesthetic criteria such as minimizing edge crossings, minimizing total edge length, maximizing angular resolution, and emphasizing the cluster structure induced by L.

Formulate precise objective functions making (i)–(iv) into a well-posed optimization problem, and provide an efficient algorithm (or prove hardness and give an approximation) for computing such layouts.

